\subsection[Challenge 22: Single particle Holevo information]{Challenge 22: Single particle Holevo information}
\textbf{Problem ID:} \texttt{Challenge\_22\_main}\\
\textbf{Primary inferred discipline:} Quantum Information, Science \& Technology\\
\textbf{Python implementation:} \texttt{challenges/22/answer.py}

\subsubsection*{Problem}
\paragraph*{Problem setup}

Optimize the Holevo information over cq states $\chi=\sum_x p_x|x\rangle\langle x|\otimes\rho_x$, where
\begin{equation}
\rho_x=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
\gamma_x \cos ^2 \theta & 0 & 0 \\
0 & \left(1-\gamma_x\right) \cos ^2 \theta & \sqrt{1-\gamma_x} \cos \theta \sin \theta e^{i \phi_x} \\
0 & \sqrt{1-\gamma_x} \cos \theta \sin \theta e^{-i \phi_x} & \sin ^2 \theta
\end{array}\right),
\end{equation}
$\{p_x\}$ is a probability distribution over $x$, and $\gamma_x$ are all real numbers in $[0,1]$. Assume that $\cos\theta\neq1$.


\paragraph*{Main problem}

Write the maximal value in terms of an optimization $\max_{x\in[0,1]} f(x)$, where $f(x)$ depends only on $x$ and nothing else. Find the function form of $f(x)$ explicitly.


{
\color{blue}
\paragraph*{Issue with the problem statement}

I find the question of the problem a bit ambiguous. It is unclear if the function $f$ is the Holevo information and what is the parameter $x$ that it takes in input. This confusion comes from the fact it is not explicitly stated and that $x$ is originally defining the classical part of the state $\chi$. 

To resolve this ambiguity I assume that $f$ is indeed the Holevo function and $x$ defines the support of the classical part of $\chi$ (i.e., it is \textbf{not} a scalar).


\paragraph*{How I would have solved the problem}
Here is a sketch of how I would solve the problem:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Define the Holevo information (source Wikipedia): 
  $$
    f(x)=S\!\left(\chi\right) - \sum_x p_x \,S\!\left(|x\rangle\langle x|\otimes\rho_x\right)=S\!\left(\chi\right) - \sum_x p_x\, S\!\left(\rho_x\right)
  $$
  where $S$ is the von Neumann entropy. The second equality follows from the additivity of $S$ and from the purity of $\ket{x}$.

  \item Diagonalize $\rho_x=\sum_{i=1}^3\lambda_i|v_i\rangle\langle v_i|$ with $\lambda_i$ being the eigenvalues and $v_i$ the eigenvectors. From the structure of $\rho_x$, one can see that it is always pure when $\gamma_x=0$ and mixed when $\gamma_x>0$. Using Mathematica, we can obtain the three eigenvalues
  $$
    \left\{
  \gamma \cos^2\theta;\ \,
  1-\gamma \cos^2\theta;\ \, 0
  \right\}
  $$

  \item Then from the definition of the Von Neumann entropy we get
  $$
    S\left(\rho_x\right) = -\sum_{i=1}^3 \lambda_i \ln(\lambda_i)
  $$
  and 
  $$
    S\left(\chi\right) = S\left(\sum_x\sum_{i=1}^3 p_x\,\lambda_i|x,v_i\rangle\langle x,v_i|\right) = -\sum_x\sum_{i=1}^3 p_x\,\lambda_i \ln(p_x\,\lambda_i)\,.
  $$

  In the particular case of all $\rho_x$ being pure, the Holevo information is $f(x) = -\sum_x p_x \ln(p_x)$.

  \item The final result can be derive using the eigenvalues found earlier and the maximum can be taken over different supports of $x$.

\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Disclosure:} I have \textbf{not} used any AI model to brainstorm my approach and have not looked at the solution prior to formulate my answer. 

}

